zl1 installer self-test, second round: cpufreq + netwatch
2026-09-23. Host-side. Device: EDL (05c6:9008, port 3-3). Nothing here touched the device.
================================================================================

This round extended scripts/host/zl1-installers-selftest.sh from 128 checks (two installers,
ssh transport) to 228 checks (four installers, two transports), and it found defects in BOTH
of the new ones. The run below is that round's, at 228 checks. The harness is now at 229
(docs 101 added the check that identifies which revision the "shipped applier" comparison is
actually reading -- the guard that turned out to be able to pass without comparing anything). Section 1 is what was wrong, section 2 is the run on the current tree,
section 3 is the same harness run against the PRE-FIX scripts from HEAD.

  $ uname -srm
Linux 5.15.0-190-generic x86_64
  $ ls -l /bin/sh
lrwxrwxrwx 1 root root 4 /bin/sh -> dash

1. the two defects
================================================================================

1a. install-cpufreq-governor.sh -- the OTHER half of the heat fix (docs 95, 96)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
What it is for: the image ships all four cores on the `performance` governor, so they sit at
1132800/1363200 kHz forever and never scale down. That is the largest avoidable heat source on
this phone, and it is half of the answer to the user's own sentence ("这台机器很容易发烫").

Three defects, all of the same kind -- an instrument whose job is to say "the heat fix is
armed" and which could not tell that it was not:

  * no `--help`. Every sibling installer in scripts/ prints a usage block; this one answered
    "unknown argument --help" and exited 2.
  * `--governor` with no value: `case ... --governor) GOVERNOR="$2"` with `set -u` aborts the
    shell --

      $ bash scripts/install-cpufreq-governor.sh --governor
      /tmp/zl1-headwt/scripts/host/../install-cpufreq-governor.sh: line 82: $2: unbound variable
      (rc=1, and the message does not say which flag was short a value)

    which is why the fix uses ${2?...}: the message names the flag.
  * THE ONE THAT MATTERS: the applier never read a write back. It counted `echo > file` as
    done whenever the echo returned, so a governor the kernel does not offer (the kernel
    answers EINVAL and leaves the old value) produced

      zl1-cpufreq: governor 'schedutil' on 0 cores
      exit 0

    -- `Result=success`, unit `active`, and all four cores still on `performance`. The whole
    point of the script is the one claim it could not check.

The read-back, and the refusal that goes with it:

GOV=${ZL1_CPUFREQ_GOVERNOR:-interactive}
n=0
bad=0
for p in /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu[0-9]*/cpufreq; do
    [ -w "$p/scaling_governor" ] || continue
    echo "$GOV" > "$p/scaling_governor" 2>/dev/null
    if [ "$(cat "$p/scaling_governor" 2>/dev/null)" = "$GOV" ]; then
        n=$((n + 1))
    else
        bad=$((bad + 1))
        echo "zl1-cpufreq: ${p%/cpufreq} did NOT take '$GOV' (reads '$(cat "$p/scaling_governor" 2>/dev/null)')"
    fi
done
logger -t zl1-cpufreq "set governor '$GOV' on $n cores, $bad did not take it (read back)"
echo "zl1-cpufreq: governor '$GOV' on $n cores ($bad did not take it)"
[ "$bad" = 0 ] || { echo "zl1-cpufreq: the heat fix is NOT armed on $bad core(s)"; exit 1; }
[ "$n" -gt 0 ] || { echo "zl1-cpufreq: no core accepted a governor at all -- the heat fix is NOT armed"; exit 1; }
exit 0
APPLIER_EOF

1b. install-netwatch-service.sh -- the only backup of the misc partition
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
This is the one script in scripts/ that READS A PARTITION at install time: misc, which the
watchdog can write "boot-recovery" into to reach TWRP. The whole block that takes the backup
was gated on

# The watchdog can ask the bootloader for recovery by writing "boot-recovery" into the
# misc partition, and the healer can toggle the USB gadget. The 2026-06-07 backup set
# has no misc image, so take one before anything could write to that partition.
MISC_OUT="/mnt/data/zl1-backups/2026-09-17-misc"
if [[ ! -f "$MISC_OUT/misc.img" ]]; then
  mkdir -p "$MISC_OUT"
  MISC_BLK="$(adb -s "$SER" shell 'readlink -f /dev/block/bootdevice/by-name/misc' | tr -d '\r')"
  if [[ -n "$MISC_BLK" && "$MISC_BLK" == /dev/block/* ]]; then
    echo "backing up misc ($MISC_BLK) -> $MISC_OUT/misc.img"
    adb -s "$SER" exec-out "cat $MISC_BLK" > "$MISC_OUT/misc.img"
    ls -l "$MISC_OUT/misc.img"
    ( cd "$MISC_OUT" && sha256sum misc.img > SHA256SUMS )

  -- `[[ -f ]]` alone, and nothing ever looked at the file again. So a run whose `exec-out`
  produced a 0-byte or truncated image (adb dropped, device unplugged, cat failed) leaves a
  file that satisfies every LATER run's `-f` test, and `sha256sum` would record the hash of
  nothing and be believed. This is the same shape the project has already been bitten by once:
  the 2026-06-07-...-exact backup set verifies 31/31 SHA256 and still cannot be mounted.

The fix: an existing image is accepted only if it is NON-EMPTY and its recorded hash still
matches (otherwise re-taken, which is the safe direction), and a freshly taken image is
cross-checked against a SECOND, independent read of the same partition before it is recorded:

misc_backup_ok() {
  [[ -s "$MISC_IMG" ]] || { echo "existing misc.img is EMPTY (size $(stat -c%s "$MISC_IMG" 2>/dev/null || echo '?'))" >&2; return 1; }
  [[ -f "$MISC_OUT/SHA256SUMS" ]] || { echo "existing misc.img has no SHA256SUMS to check it against" >&2; return 1; }
  ( cd "$MISC_OUT" && sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS >/dev/null 2>&1 ) \
    || { echo "existing misc.img FAILS its recorded SHA256 (it is not the image it claims to be)" >&2; return 1; }
  return 0
}


